  "body": "When looking at a set of numbers, extreme values can skew the average. A box plot uses the middle half of the data to draw a box, marked by the first and third quartiles. The distance between these two quartiles is called the interquartile range, or IQR for short.\n\nLook at the box plot in [[visual:1]] to see how fences mark the limits of expected values. We find the IQR by subtracting the first quartile from the third quartile. Multiplying this range by 1.5 gives us a boundary distance.\n\nAdding this scaled distance to the third quartile creates an upper fence, while subtracting it from the first quartile creates a lower fence. Any data point that lies beyond these fences is flagged as an outlier. In our dataset, the number 40 sits far past the upper fence and is marked separately.\n\nThis method keeps extreme spikes from distorting the main picture of our data. The whiskers of the plot then stretch only to the last real data points that sit safely inside the fences.",
